WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.431
Secure build pipelines help enforce repeatable controls without relying on memory.

2
00:00:05.431 --> 00:00:20.290
Source control protections, branch protection concepts, required code review, build integrity checks, artifact handling, and environment separation all reduce the chance that unreviewed or untrusted changes reach production.

3
00:00:20.290 --> 00:00:27.230
Automation is useful because it applies common checks consistently, but it should not hide accountability.

4
00:00:27.230 --> 00:00:38.167
CI/CD security also includes infrastructure-as-code review, configuration management, registry or artifact repository controls, secret handling, and policy gates.

5
00:00:38.167 --> 00:00:47.143
Build systems often have access to source code, deployment credentials, package registries, signing keys, and production configuration.

6
00:00:47.143 --> 00:00:51.140
That makes the pipeline part of the product security boundary.

7
00:00:51.140 --> 00:00:55.440
Protecting the pipeline protects the software that comes out of it.

8
00:00:55.440 --> 00:01:00.192
Release control is about making risk visible before change reaches users.

9
00:01:00.192 --> 00:01:13.995
Deployment approvals, release risk assessments, audit trails, and separation between development, testing, staging, and production environments help teams understand what is changing and who accepted the risk.

10
00:01:13.995 --> 00:01:22.519
Not every release needs a heavy process, but high-impact changes should have more evidence and review than routine low-risk updates.

11
00:01:22.519 --> 00:01:31.117
Rollback planning gives teams a planned way to recover if a release causes unacceptable security, operational, or functional impact.

12
00:01:31.117 --> 00:01:42.356
A secure release process asks whether the artifact is known, the configuration is reviewed, the deployment path is controlled, monitoring is ready, and recovery is practical.

13
00:01:42.356 --> 00:01:49.296
Pipelines should enforce checks while still leaving humans responsible for risk decisions and exceptions.
